From aee32fed3376b899095e86000220fbe0d06362f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Olivier Pavilla <olivier.pavilla@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 15:46:20 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] tests: bound concurrent-stress teardown check for GCC >=
 14

GCC 14/15 at -O2 (meson --buildtype=release, e.g. Ubuntu 26.04 aarch64
gcc 15.2) warns -Wstringop-overflow on the final teardown_completed
load in run_rep(): after the pthread calls it can no longer prove
rep.n_streams <= MAX_STREAMS, although main() already rejects larger
values. Bound the loop explicitly. No behaviour change.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FQfobs28ekFzBoyDd2gygv
---
 tests/concurrent-stress.c | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/tests/concurrent-stress.c b/tests/concurrent-stress.c
index 880ae92..dd99354 100644
--- a/tests/concurrent-stress.c
+++ b/tests/concurrent-stress.c
@@ -1979,7 +1979,10 @@ static void run_rep(void)
     atomic_store_explicit(&rep_done, true, memory_order_release);
     assert(pthread_join(threads[0], NULL) == 0);
 
-    for (unsigned i = 0; i < rep.n_streams; i++) {
+    /* main() rejects n_streams > MAX_STREAMS; the explicit bound only
+     * keeps GCC >= 14 at -O2 from assuming an index past streams[] after
+     * the thread calls above (false -Wstringop-overflow on this load). */
+    for (unsigned i = 0; i < rep.n_streams && i < MAX_STREAMS; i++) {
         struct stream *s = &rep.streams[i];
 
         assert(atomic_load(&s->teardown_completed));
